rror in approximating the  true top hat function and the reason I  think the way I want to explain this to  you is this is following so the Fourier  series can approximate these these  continuous functions when you have a  discontinuous function sines and cosines  are are continuous right so sines in  code  signs are kind of smooth continuous  functions they don't have sharp corners  or jumps and things like that and so to  approximate something that has a  discontinuous jump essentially what what  this means is that this discontinuous  jump contains all frequencies all  infinitely many higher and higher and  higher frequency signs and co-signs need  to be added up in a perfect mixture to  approximate this the steep front here  this cliff where where the function  jumps from zero to one and if I truncate  my and that when they add up to form  that cliff the ups and the downs of the  different frequency signs and co-signs  are kind of cancelling out perfectly to  just walk up instantaneously and then  stay flat and when I truncate that for  you series in this case at 100 modes I'm  not getting that perfect cancellation of  sines and cosines that I would that I  would have and so now you see the  artifacts because of that imperfect  cancellation I need all of the terms in  my series to get rid of these Gibbs  phenomenon and this is something you're  always going to see when you approximate  discontinuous functions using a finite  truncated Fourier series so for example  if I do this numerically you know in in  Python then I'm gonna get this Gibbs  phenomenon unless I use exactly the same  number of modes as they as I have points  in my in my vector okay  and that's actually the only catch here  is that if I had let's say N equals 1024  points in my discrete representation of  this data then if I had 512 or n over 2  modes this Gibbs phenomenon would look  like it went away  I would I would have a Fourier series  approximation on this grid that is you  know is a perfect representation of my  function but what would happen if you  zoomed in between your grid you would  see this for this Gibbs phenomenon and  essentially what you would find is that  you were just sampling this only at the  points where it intersected exactly with  this function ok so we're gonna get more  into this later I'll tell you more about  Gibson  and especially how this relates to the  fast Fourier transform and the discrete  Fourier transform which is how you  actually compute this on data  efficiently but I just wanted to point  out that this is a big kind of issue  okay and even though we showed that you  can compute the Fourier series of this  triangular hat function if I use the  Fourier series to approximate the  derivative of this which is a top hat  function then the derivative is gonna  have this Gibbs phenomenon so so that's  something that you're you're always  gonna have to look out for is this kind  of ringing Gibbs phenomenon but now  you'll you'll know what it is when you  see it okay and then the last thing I  want to show you here is you can turn  this into a movie where you iteratively  increase the order of this approximation  from K equals 1 to 2 to 3 all the way up  to 100 and you can see how the Gibbs  phenomenon evolves so this is kind of a  cool movie here see if I can play it ok  so it's going to start over in a minute  ok so this is the Gibbs phenomenon as  you increase the order of your  approximation from from low order from a  very coarse approximation to a very high  approximation you can see in the low  order it starts off just being kind of  one cosine function that's a poor  approximation and as you add and add and  add more cosines you see that this Gibbs  phenomena starts to localize out at the  corners so it's doing a better and  better job in the middle and that gives  phenomena that ringing is localizing out  to those points of discontinuity and if  you increased this n higher again to the  number of grid points divided by two  eventually it would look like it goes  away and it's a perfect approximation  but if you doubled your resolution and  zoomed in you'd see that they're still  ringing  you're just a liasing that raining ok  great so something to look out for and  soon we're gonna start talking about the  fast Fourier transform and the discrete  Fourier transform again those are how to  efficiently compute these in a computer  and and we'll we'll use these to  approximate partial differential  equations and remember when you're  approximating derivatives you have to  look out for for Gibbs phenomenon ok  thank you

